  {
   "slug": "kathy-velikov",
   "Name": "Kathy Velikov, FAIA",
   "Role": "Director, RVTR. Professor of Architecture and Associate Dean for Research and Creative Practice, Taubman College",
   "Group": "Director",
   "Years": "2007–",
   "Portrait": {
    "url": "https://framerusercontent.com/images/LvW5dCuxP2UtK1hg1qeCjmpE1hM.jpg",
    "alt": "Kathy Velikov"
   },
   "Short Line": "Professor of Architecture and Associate Dean for Research and Creative Practice at Taubman College. Elevated to the AIA College of Fellows in 2025.",
   "Bio": "Kathy Velikov is an architect, researcher and educator, and a director of RVTR, the research lab she co-founded in Toronto in 2007 with Geoffrey Thün, Colin Ripley and Paul Raff. She is Professor of Architecture and Associate Dean for Research and Creative Practice at Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning, University of Michigan, and directs the Institute for Advanced Construction Futures, a University of Michigan Impact Institute funded in January 2026 to advance low-carbon construction. She is licensed in Michigan and Ontario. In 2025 she was elevated to the College of Fellows of the American Institute of Architects.\nHer work is practice-based design research, in which new knowledge comes from design propositions and prototypes, developed through speculation, prototyping and visualization. At the material scale she prototypes building skins that integrate environmental sensing, kinetic action and responsiveness. At the territorial scale she uses urban analysis to build scenarios for decarbonized futures and new social infrastructures. With RVTR she was architect of record for North House, the solar-powered prototype for northern climates built for the 2009 U.S. Department of Energy Solar Decathlon; co-led Resonant Chamber, a rigid-origami acoustic envelope that received a U.S. patent in 2016; and co-authored Infra Eco Logi Urbanism: A Project for the Great Lakes Megaregion (Park Books, 2015). RVTR was part of the EXTRACTION team at the Canadian Pavilion, 2016 Venice Architecture Biennale. Her research has been funded by NSF, the National Fish and Wildlife Foundation, the U.S. Department of Transportation, SSHRC, Ford and Guardian Industries. She has published two books, more than twenty book chapters, thirty journal articles and forty conference papers.\nShe was President of ACADIA, the Association for Computer Aided Design in Architecture, from 2018 to 2020 and Vice President from 2021 to 2022. As President she created the ACADIA Diversity Committee and updated its publication ethics. She was Technical Co-Chair of ACADIA 2016, Posthuman Frontiers, hosted at the University of Michigan, and co-edited its proceedings. Since 2022 she has been External Examiner for the MArch Architectural Design (B-Pro) program at The Bartlett, UCL. She was Visiting Guest Professor at California College of the Arts in 2013 and Visiting Professor at Ryerson University in 2010, and is an invited guest critic at schools including Harvard GSD, Princeton, MIT and The Bartlett; in 2016 she spoke at the Realities and Realms Symposium at Harvard GSD. She co-edited Ambiguous Territory: Architecture, Landscape, and the Postnatural (Actar, 2022) and co-curated its exhibition, shown at four galleries from 2017 to 2019.\nAt Taubman College she teaches thesis and design studios and seminars in ecology and technology. She has taught more than 800 architecture students and employed more than 60 graduate research assistants. Her housing studio received the 2020 AIA/ACSA Housing Design Education Award, and in 2023 ACADIA gave her its Society Award for Leadership. She holds a B.ES and a B.Arch from the University of Waterloo and an MA in History of Art and Architecture from the University of Toronto.",
   "Appointments": "Professor of Architecture, Taubman College, University of Michigan, 2021–\nAssociate Dean for Research and Creative Practice, Taubman College, 2021–\nDirector, Institute for Advanced Construction Futures, University of Michigan, 2026–\nDirector, RVTR (research lab), Ann Arbor, 2017–\nFounding Partner and Director, RVTR Inc, Ann Arbor and Toronto, 2007–2017\nAssociate Professor, Taubman College, 2015–2021; Assistant Professor, 2009–2015\nAssistant Professor, University of Waterloo, 2007–2009\nWillard A. Oberdick Fellow, Taubman College, 2006–2007\nLicensed architect: Michigan (since 2012), Ontario (since 2002)",
   "Visiting": "External Examiner, MArch Architectural Design (B-Pro), The Bartlett, UCL, 2022–\nProgram Advisory Board, Florida Atlantic University School of Architecture, 2023–\nDesign Research Workshop, M.Arch, Syracuse University, 2022\nVisiting Guest Professor, Summer Studio, California College of the Arts, 2013\nVisiting Professor, MArch Summer Studio, Ryerson University (now Toronto Metropolitan University), 2010\nSpeaker, Realities and Realms Symposium, Harvard GSD, 2016\nInvited guest critic: Harvard GSD, Princeton, MIT, The Bartlett, Carnegie Mellon, Cornell Tech, University of Pennsylvania Weitzman, University of Virginia, University of Chicago, among others",
   "Leadership": "ACADIA: Board of Directors, 2016–2017; President, 2018–2020; Vice President, 2021–2022\nFounder of the ACADIA Diversity Committee\nTechnical Co-Chair, ACADIA 2016, Posthuman Frontiers, University of Michigan; co-editor of the proceedings and projects catalog\nCo-organizer, LafargeHolcim Foundation 5th International Forum, Infrastructure Space, Detroit, 2016\nCo-curator, Ambiguous Territory, exhibition and symposium, 2017–2019; co-editor of the book, Actar, 2022\nFellow, Big Ten Academic Alliance Academic Leadership Program, 2022–2023\nPeer reviewer for IJAC, JAE, Architectural Science Review, ACADIA and FABRICATE; juror, eVolo Skyscraper Competition and ACSA Steel Design Competition",
   "Honours": "2026 Tulane Prize Honorable Achievement, Innovative Studio Pedagogy, studio Arid Logics\n2025 Fellow, American Institute of Architects (FAIA)\n2023 ACADIA Society Award for Leadership\n2020 AIA/ACSA Housing Design Education Award, studio New Domesticities New Collectivities\n2020 TAD Award for Research Contribution, \"Climate Measures\"\n2017 AZ Award, Social Good, EXTRACTION (with OPSYS)\n2017 CSLA National Award of Excellence, EXTRACTION\n2016 Architect R+D Awards, Honorable Mention, Infundibuliforms\n2013 Architizer A+ Awards, Architecture + Sound, Jury Award and Popular Choice Award, Resonant Chamber\n2013 JAE Best Design as Scholarship Article Award, \"Thick Air\"\n2012 ACSA Faculty Design Award, The Stratus Project\n2011 RAIC Award of Excellence for Innovation in the Practice of Architecture, North House\n2010 Architect R+D Award of Excellence, North House\n2010 OAA Design Excellence Award, North House\n2009 Professional Prix de Rome in Architecture, Canada Council for the Arts (RVTR)\n2008 Young Architects Forum Award, Architectural League of New York",
