  {
   "slug": "geoffrey-thun",
   "Name": "Geoffrey Thün",
   "Role": "Director of Design Research, RVTR. Professor of Architecture, Taubman College; Associate Vice President for Research, University of Michigan",
   "Group": "Director",
   "Years": "2007–",
   "Portrait": {
    "url": "https://framerusercontent.com/images/Auevx9vaTUNIFgEkoLm9QkN135c.jpg",
    "alt": "Geoffrey Thün"
   },
   "Short Line": "Professor of Architecture at Taubman College and Associate Vice President for Research at the University of Michigan.",
   "Bio": "Geoffrey Thün is a designer, researcher and educator, and a director of RVTR, which he co-founded in Toronto in 2007 with Kathy Velikov, Colin Ripley and Paul Raff; he leads the lab's design research. He is Professor of Architecture at Taubman College of Architecture and Urban Planning and Associate Vice President for Research: Social Sciences, Humanities, and the Arts at the University of Michigan, where he is the research office's liaison to those units. He is also co-director of the university's Urban Collaboratory.\nHis research applies complex-systems approaches across scales: regional territories and city planning, high-performance buildings, and full-scale prototypes of responsive, kinetic building envelopes. He was faculty lead and principal investigator for North House, the Team North entry to the 2009 U.S. Department of Energy Solar Decathlon, and co-led The Stratus Project and Resonant Chamber, two prototypes of responsive interior envelopes. With Kathy Velikov and Colin Ripley he developed Conduit Urbanism and Infra Eco Logi Urbanism, design research on the Great Lakes Megaregion published by Park Books in 2015 and exhibited in Montreal, Toronto, New Haven, Ann Arbor and Knoxville. He was principal investigator for M-NEX, a Detroit project funded in 2018 through the Belmont Forum's SUGI Food-Water-Energy Nexus program, and is co-principal investigator for the University of Michigan component of NextHouse, the Purdue-led factory-built housing initiative that received a National Science Foundation Engines Development Award in 2024. In 2024 he gave the keynote \"Responsive Envelopes: Paradigms and Potentials\" at the Facade Tectonics World Congress.\nAt Taubman College he was Associate Dean for Research and Creative Practice from 2014 to 2020 and Senior Associate Dean from 2020 to 2021. He co-organized ACADIA 2016, Posthuman Frontiers, at the University of Michigan, co-edited its proceedings and catalog, and co-designed its exhibition with Matias del Campo. With Dan McTavish he planned and designed the display for New Brutalist Image 1949–55 at Tate Britain (2014–2015). He taught at the University of Waterloo from 2006 to 2009, was Adjunct Associate Professor at Ryerson University from 2011 to 2015, and has held visiting positions at California College of the Arts, Université du Québec à Montréal, Dalhousie University, Keio University and the International Solar University in Switzerland. He teaches design studios and courses on urban systems, site operations and material systems.\nHe shared in RVTR's recognition, including the 2009 Professional Prix de Rome in Architecture, the 2010 Architect R+D Award of Excellence and the 2011 RAIC Award of Excellence for Innovation, and received the University of Waterloo Outstanding Faculty Researcher Award in 2008. Before RVTR he worked at Dereck Revington Studio and at Baird Sampson Neuert Architects, where he was central to the French River Visitor Centre. He holds a B.A. in Sociology from the University of Western Ontario, a B.ES and a B.Arch from the University of Waterloo, and a Master of Urban Design from the University of Toronto.",
   "Appointments": "Professor of Architecture, Taubman College, University of Michigan, 2020–\nAssociate Vice President for Research: Social Sciences, Humanities, and the Arts, University of Michigan, 2021–\nDirector of Design Research, RVTR, 2007–\nCo-director, Urban Collaboratory, University of Michigan\nSenior Associate Dean for Research and Creative Practice, Taubman College, 2020–2021\nAssociate Dean for Research and Creative Practice, Taubman College, 2014–2020\nAssociate Professor, Taubman College, 2009–2020\nAssistant Professor, University of Waterloo, 2006–2009\nAdjunct Associate Professor, Ryerson University, 2011–2015\nLEED AP since 2004",
   "Visiting": "Keio University (online), 2021\nAdjunct and external reader, Dalhousie University, 2012–2017\nCalifornia College of the Arts, Summer 2013\nUniversité du Québec à Montréal, Winter 2013\nInternational Solar University, Falera, Switzerland, Summer 2011\nRyerson University, Summer 2010",
   "Leadership": "Co-organizer, ACADIA 2016, Posthuman Frontiers, University of Michigan; co-editor of the proceedings and catalog; exhibition co-designer with Matias del Campo\nPrincipal investigator, M-NEX, Belmont Forum SUGI Food-Water-Energy Nexus, 2018\nFaculty lead and principal investigator, North House, Team North, 2009 Solar Decathlon\nModerator, \"Territorial Scale\" workshop, LafargeHolcim Foundation 5th International Forum, Detroit, 2016\nCo-founder, Metropolitan Futures Group, University of Michigan\nSpatial planning and display design with Dan McTavish, New Brutalist Image 1949–55, Tate Britain, 2014–2015\nKeynote, Facade Tectonics 2024 World Congress",
   "Honours": "2017 AZ Award, Social Good, EXTRACTION\n2016 Architect R+D Awards, Honorable Mention, Infundibuliforms\n2013 Architizer A+ Awards, Architecture + Sound, Jury Award and Popular Choice Award, Resonant Chamber\n2013 JAE Best Design as Scholarship Article Award, \"Thick Air\"\n2012 ACSA Faculty Design Award, The Stratus Project\n2011 RAIC Award of Excellence for Innovation in Architecture, North House\n2010 Architect R+D Award of Excellence, North House\n2009 Professional Prix de Rome in Architecture, Canada Council for the Arts (RVTR)\n2008 University of Waterloo Outstanding Faculty Researcher Award\n2008 Young Architects Forum Award, Architectural League of New York",
